      <h1>How to Change Course/Department in Nigerian Universities</h1>
      <p>Academic Requirements, Step-by-Step Procedure & Eligibility Guidelines</p>
    </section>

    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-txt">
        <h2>General Transfer Requirements</h2>
        <ul class="child-ul">
          <li><strong>Academic Performance:</strong> Candidates usually require a strong CGPA (typically 3.00 or higher on a 5.0 scale).</li>
          <li><strong>Subject Combination Match:</strong> Your O'Level grades and initial UTME subject combination must satisfy the requirements of the new department.</li>
          <li><strong>Capacity Constraints:</strong> Transfers depend on available vacant slots in the target department.</li>
          <li><strong>Level Restrictions:</strong> Most institutions process departmental transfers strictly after 100-Level (first year).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="section-txt">
        <h2>Step-by-Step Application Procedure</h2>
        <ul class="child-ul">
          <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Consult your academic adviser and inquire at the academic affairs division.</li>
          <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Obtain the official Change of Course/Department form from the portal or bursary.</li>
          <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Pay the required administrative processing fee.</li>
          <li><strong>Step 4:</strong> Submit completed forms alongside academic transcripts for HOD signatures.</li>
          <li><strong>Step 5:</strong> Receive final approval notification from the Senate/Admissions office.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="section-txt">
        <h2>Participating Universities</h2>
        <p>Institutions offering departmental transfer processes include UNILAG, UNN, ABU, UNILORIN, OAU, Covenant University, and AE-FUNAI.</p>
